iv> <div class="ecom-column ecom-core core__column--last ecom-0mvxy429lrt"><div class="core__column--wrapper" id="ecom-0mvxy429lrt"><div class="ecom-column__overlay"><div class="ecom-overlay"></div></div><div class="core__blocks"><div class="core__blocks--body"><div class="ecom-block ecom-core core__block elmspace ecom-zbdqym2rus"><div class="ecom-row ecom-core core__block ecom-vv5rrjz1oq" data-id="ecom-vv5rrjz1oq" style="z-index: inherit;"><div class="core__row--columns"><div class="ecom-column ecom-core core__column--first core__column--last ecom-00h8nxqisdmu"><div class="core__column--wrapper" id="ecom-00h8nxqisdmu"><div class="ecom-column__overlay"><div class="ecom-overlay"></div></div><div class="core__blocks"><div class="core__blocks--body"><div class="ecom-block ecom-core core__block ecom-ejygvwpmdh"><div class="ecom__element ecom-element element__text ecom-type-deffault" section_id="ecom-dek70lc05f5" deep="2"><div class="text-content ecom-html has-drop-cap-view-framed"><div>The world has lost its way with water. <a href="https://www.livescience.com/29673-how-much-water-on-earth.html" target="_blank">Less than 1 %</a> of the earth’s freshwater is actually 
